WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.603
When you hear agentic coding, you might think Vibe coding.

2
00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:06.973
Let's compare the two and see how Spec-Driven Development

3
00:00:07.073 --> 00:00:10.977
gives better results by bringing back engineering.

4
00:00:10.977 --> 00:00:13.179
Vibe coding gives quick results.

5
00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:15.982
You write a prompt describing what you want,

6
00:00:15.982 --> 00:00:18.051
like, create me a button

7
00:00:18.151 --> 00:00:21.354
and hope for the best. Then you look at the result.

8
00:00:21.821 --> 00:00:25.625
That's a big button. It's kind of close,

9
00:00:25.625 --> 00:00:27.627
but off on some important things.

10
00:00:28.028 --> 00:00:30.130
So you point out the mistakes to the agent,

11
00:00:30.130 --> 00:00:34.334
it tries again and so on until you are satisfied.

12
00:00:34.667 --> 00:00:36.469
As a result, you will end up

13
00:00:36.469 --> 00:00:38.271
with a long dialogue with the agent,

14
00:00:38.571 --> 00:00:41.474
the history of which will not even be saved.

15
00:00:42.175 --> 00:00:44.477
This approach works okay for a button,

16
00:00:44.677 --> 00:00:48.181
but it doesn't scale to a large ongoing project.

17
00:00:48.648 --> 00:00:50.950
While high-level prompts are fast,

18
00:00:50.950 --> 00:00:55.950
they lead to disposable code and mounting technical debt. We need engineering.

19
00:00:57.457 --> 00:01:02.362
A well-maintained specification that creates a permanent technical artifact.

20
00:01:02.896 --> 00:01:05.698
Spec-Driven Development is the professional response

21
00:01:05.899 --> 00:01:09.502
to the chaos of unsupervised AI generation.

22
00:01:09.903 --> 00:01:12.705
It is a paradigm shift where the Specification,

23
00:01:13.206 --> 00:01:15.208
explaining the what and why,

24
00:01:15.608 --> 00:01:18.812
is decoupled from the Implementation, the how.

25
00:01:18.978 --> 00:01:21.681
With specs, we get a contract

26
00:01:21.781 --> 00:01:25.051
between the humans, but also with the agent.

27
00:01:25.652 --> 00:01:28.254
Your main task as the human now shifts.

28
00:01:28.655 --> 00:01:30.757
Learn how to convert your intentions

29
00:01:30.857 --> 00:01:33.159
into clear specifications.

30
00:01:33.526 --> 00:01:36.329
Spec-driven development with agentic coding assistance

31
00:01:36.329 --> 00:01:38.731
has three main benefits.

32
00:01:39.232 --> 00:01:42.335
First, you're able to control large code changes

33
00:01:42.335 --> 00:01:44.237
with small changes to the spec.

34
00:01:44.771 --> 00:01:46.873
A few sentences in the spec

35
00:01:46.873 --> 00:01:49.375
outlining the look and feel of the app

36
00:01:49.375 --> 00:01:52.278
might translate to hundreds of lines of CSS.

37
00:01:52.579 --> 00:01:54.781
This spec-driven approach reduces

38
00:01:54.781 --> 00:01:57.283
the cognitive overhead needed for working

39
00:01:57.283 --> 00:01:59.886
with these ultra-fast coding agents.

40
00:02:00.153 --> 00:02:03.957
Second, specs eliminate the context decay problem

41
00:02:04.157 --> 00:02:06.759
that derails multi-turn agent sessions.

42
00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:09.162
As you work with your coding agent,

43
00:02:09.362 --> 00:02:11.464
its context window will fill up,

44
00:02:11.965 --> 00:02:13.967
often leading to more mistakes

45
00:02:14.067 --> 00:02:17.937
as the agent tries to cope with a full working memory.

46
00:02:18.238 --> 00:02:21.641
Specs persist between sessions and even agents,

47
00:02:21.641 --> 00:02:25.145
anchoring the agent to the core context needed

48
00:02:25.145 --> 00:02:28.348
to work in a code base and implement a feature.

49
00:02:28.948 --> 00:02:31.251
Third, specs improve intent fidelity,

50
00:02:31.651 --> 00:02:35.054
meaning that the agent is more likely to produce code

51
00:02:35.054 --> 00:02:36.656
that matches your goals.

52
00:02:37.056 --> 00:02:40.560
That's because specs force you to define the problem,

53
00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:43.863
success criteria, constraints, user flows,

54
00:02:44.164 --> 00:02:47.834
and so on before the agent starts generating code.

55
00:02:48.268 --> 00:02:51.070
Specs are a key differentiator between vibe coding,

56
00:02:51.171 --> 00:02:54.674
some slop, and engineering a viable software product.

57
00:02:55.475 --> 00:02:58.278
Whether you are starting a new project from scratch

58
00:02:58.378 --> 00:03:01.080
or want to implement the SDD approach

59
00:03:01.181 --> 00:03:04.050
into a project that has been running for years,

60
00:03:04.250 --> 00:03:07.754
specs help solve the drift and productivity problems.

61
00:03:08.488 --> 00:03:10.790
As a comparison, think of compilers,

62
00:03:11.191 --> 00:03:14.294
which convert understandable source code

63
00:03:14.294 --> 00:03:16.196
into machine code.

64
00:03:16.796 --> 00:03:19.499
SDD guides the agent and prompts,

65
00:03:19.499 --> 00:03:21.701
converting specs into source code.

66
00:03:22.001 --> 00:03:24.971
Even better, the specs are in a human language,

67
00:03:25.471 --> 00:03:27.073
making it easy for stakeholders.

68
00:03:27.674 --> 00:03:29.976
Spec-driven development has taken off recently

69
00:03:30.076 --> 00:03:33.580
as a solution to concerns about productivity.

70
00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:37.584
Multiple SDD projects, tools built around spec authoring,

71
00:03:37.684 --> 00:03:39.886
conference talks on capturing intent.

72
00:03:40.086 --> 00:03:43.990
This is all part of a broader push to bring engineering,

73
00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:48.494
lessons learned from the software development life cycle, to agentic coding.

74
00:03:48.962 --> 00:03:53.366
Spec-Driven Development is used with Coding Agents, not simple chatbots.

75
00:03:53.866 --> 00:03:56.970
A chatbot can talk about code, but the chatbot

76
00:03:57.670 --> 00:04:00.773
doesn't have access to your project's code

77
00:04:00.873 --> 00:04:02.775
nor tools you have installed.

78
00:04:02.842 --> 00:04:07.842
It just responds to your prompts. Agents are different.

79
00:04:07.847 --> 00:04:09.916
They take your prompt, make a

80
00:04:09.916 --> 00:04:12.318
plan, and guide themselves to a result

81
00:04:12.318 --> 00:04:14.621
using reasoning along the way.

82
00:04:14.988 --> 00:04:18.258
Importantly, agents have access to your code base

83
00:04:18.658 --> 00:04:20.360
and your development tools.

84
00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:23.663
In the SDD workflow, we treat these agents

85
00:04:23.663 --> 00:04:25.965
as highly capable pair programmers.

86
00:04:26.366 --> 00:04:29.369
They provide the technical knowledge and the speed,

87
00:04:29.569 --> 00:04:33.172
and you, the senior architect, provide the blueprints.

88
00:04:33.673 --> 00:04:36.175
As we move forward in this course, remember,

89
00:04:36.276 --> 00:04:38.878
the agent is the muscle, but the SPEC is the brain.

90
00:04:39.178 --> 00:04:42.582
With practice, you ensure that the software produced

91
00:04:42.682 --> 00:04:47.153
is not just functional, but is aligned with your long-term goals.
